h('Abstract')
p('Drug-drug interactions (DDIs) can affect the safety and effectiveness of combination therapy. Inspired by multimodal DDI research, this study evaluates whether drug-target annotations complement molecular fingerprints when predicting the type of a known interaction. We assembled a reproducible, class-capped sample of 9,320 annotated pairs from eight common event classes in a public benchmark and matched the drugs to a separate public drug-target table. A 512-bit Morgan fingerprint and binary protein-target indicators were combined in an XGBoost classifier; linear support vector machines and random forests provided comparators. Three resampling seeds were used to evaluate random held-out pairs, pairs with one unseen drug and pairs with two unseen drugs. The primary metric was macro-averaged F1. For XGBoost, structure-plus-target macro-F1 was '+val('random','xgboost','structure_target')+' under random splitting, '+val('cold_s1','xgboost','structure_target')+' with one unseen drug, and '+val('cold_s2','xgboost','structure_target')+' with two unseen drugs. Target-only performance under the latter split was '+val('cold_s2','xgboost','target')+'. These experiments demonstrate substantial split sensitivity and do not show a universal advantage from simple feature concatenation. The dataset contains interacting pairs only; predictions classify an annotated event rather than establish whether a pair is safe or interacts. External and prospective validation are necessary.')
p('<b>Keywords:</b> drug-drug interaction; machine learning; XGBoost; molecular fingerprint; drug target; cold start.')
h('1. Introduction')
p('Combinations of medicines may alter pharmacological exposure or effects and can introduce adverse drug reactions. This is relevant to oncology, where antineoplastic and supportive medicines may be used together; however, an interaction record, an experimentally synergistic combination and a clinical outcome are different endpoints. OrganADR, for example, considers organ-level adverse reactions in combination pharmacotherapy [1], whereas an anticancer-focused DDI study used a separate model and dataset [16]. Neither establishes the effectiveness or safety of a pair in a particular patient.')
p('Drug-drug interaction (DDI) prediction can be formulated in multiple ways: detection of a possible interaction, classification of its event type, prediction of severity, or estimation of a quantitative pharmacokinetic change. The choice determines the necessary labels and the interpretation of model outputs [4,8,18]. The present investigation addresses event classification only: given a pair already annotated as interacting, it predicts one of eight selected event IDs. Because no non-interacting examples are included, the model cannot determine whether a pair interacts or is safe.')
p('Our objective is a transparent comparative course study using public benchmark data. We compare support vector machines, random forests and XGBoost under identical feature representations and partitions, and examine how results change when labelled interactions for one or both test drugs are unavailable. The design is inspired by questions raised in CSK-DDI [19] and DDI-Ben [20], while deliberately using a smaller, independently implemented classical machine-learning workflow. It is not a reproduction of either architecture.')
h('2. Literature Review')
p('Twenty relevant PubMed-indexed records were selected for this narrative review, including original studies and reviews. All 20 abstracts and metadata were examined; for six, relevant full-text sections or the user-provided article were also available. This targeted selection is not a systematic review, and article-level performance claims are not treated as directly comparable when datasets, endpoints and evaluation splits differ. A separate reading record identifies the material accessed for each article.')
h2('2.1 Molecular and biomedical representations')
p('Established event-prediction studies include DeepDDI and DDIMDL: the former used structural information to predict interaction descriptions, while the latter combined substructure, target, enzyme and pathway information across 65 event categories [21,22]. Reviews describe traditional machine learning, deep learning, network and text-based methods as complementary approaches rather than a single uniform task [4,8,10,18]. More recently, T-DDI used explicit physicochemical descriptors and uncertainty-aware prediction for multi-class events [3]. Its authors evaluated a different, substantially larger dataset; its reported scores cannot be compared directly with the present results.')
p('An alternative is to incorporate relationships among drugs and biomedical entities. EmerGNN represents interactions through paths in a biomedical network [2], HetDDI combines molecular and heterogeneous graph information through pretraining [9], and ACDGNN uses cross-domain propagation among drug-related entities [11]. CSK-DDI combines motif, molecular graph and knowledge-graph representations with cross-attention and a cold-start-oriented strategy [19]. These studies motivate adding context beyond chemical structure, but a binary vector of annotated drug targets is only a simple pharmacological feature: it is not a learned knowledge graph and does not reproduce these models.')
h2('2.2 Multimodal learning, novelty and distribution shift')
p('Recent work extends multimodal representations in different directions. MHAFR-DDI combines sequence, two-dimensional structure and three-dimensional conformation [6]; TRACE incorporates chemical knowledge into graph-transformer learning [7]; IAMV-DDI combines two- and three-dimensional information with drug-pair attention [13]. FGSGT-DDI adds language-model-derived functional-group semantics [14]. Their reported performance is specific to their own benchmarks, and feature attribution or attention can generate mechanistic hypotheses without establishing biological causality.')
p('Generalisation is an increasingly important evaluation question. DDI-Ben found that representative methods can deteriorate under simulated drug-distribution changes [20]. BiG-Stack considered a scaffold-disjoint single-cold-start setting and examined features that may act as postmarket proxies [15], while SF-Rx considered scaffold-based evaluation and interaction directionality [17]. ProAlign-DDI addresses a different challenge: event categories unseen during training [12]. This distinction matters because our S1 and S2 settings hold out drug identities, not classes, scaffolds or a later time period.')
h2('2.3 Translational context and research gap')
p('Quantitative pharmacokinetic predictions can address a different translational question from assigning event labels. PK-DDIP estimated exposure changes from drug-label data and used subsequent labels and patient results in external evaluation [5]. OrganADR considered organ-related adverse reactions [1], and SMVSNN studied an anticancer dataset [16]. These studies illustrate potential applications without making our general benchmark disease-specific. We assess whether a modest, reproducible comparison of chemical fingerprints and target annotations remains informative when the train-test relationship changes, while recording the limitations of the data and evaluation.')
h('3. Research Questions and Objectives')
p('<b>RQ1:</b> How accurately can XGBoost classify eight common DDI event IDs among already annotated interacting pairs? <b>RQ2:</b> Does adding drug-target indicators to chemical fingerprints improve performance over either representation alone? <b>RQ3:</b> How does performance change when one or both drugs have no training-pair labels? Secondary objectives are to compare the principal model with two common machine learning baselines and identify limitations that matter for drug-discovery use.')
h('4. Methods and Materials')
h2('4.1 Study design and sources')
p('The interaction and SMILES files were downloaded from the CSK-DDI public code repository [19], based on the Deng benchmark [21]. The combined training, validation and test files of the first provided split contained 37,264 records and 65 numeric event classes. We recombined those files before making our own partitions, so our splits and task are not the original article\'s benchmark. Each unordered drug pair occurred once in this source snapshot. Drug-target protein annotations came from the public DSimSynergy data repository [23], joined using DrugBank identifiers. The input files and SHA-256 digests are recorded in the project source.')
p('The eight most frequent source classes (IDs 0-7) were selected. Records lacking parseable structures were removed; then at most 1,200 rows per class were sampled with seed 8122, yielding 9,320 records involving 551 distinct drugs. Class counts ranged from 1,086 to 1,200. This intentional rebalancing is useful for a manageable comparison but changes event prevalence and excludes 57 less common classes. It cannot represent the frequency or severity of interactions in clinical practice.')
fig('workflow.png',16,2.9,'Figure 1. Workflow from public sources to representations, partitions and evaluation. “New” drugs have no labelled interaction in the corresponding training partition; their structures and target annotations remain available.')
h2('4.2 Drug representations and model')
p('Each drug SMILES was converted using RDKit [26] to a radius-2 Morgan fingerprint with 512 binary bits. The target table maps DrugBank IDs to UniProt proteins; a binary indicator vector records each available protein annotation. Across the selected 551 drugs, 536 had at least one target annotation; missing target data were encoded as an all-zero vector, not asserted to mean no biological targets. The protein vocabulary contains 1,519 proteins from annotated source drugs with valid structures and was assembled without DDI labels. Both drugs retain their source column order, and their representations are concatenated, preserving possible direction-specific event information.')
p('Three input configurations were evaluated: structure only, target indicators only, and their concatenation. No DDI edges were included as explicit input features. Nonetheless, the target table and benchmark can share DrugBank-derived information; their construction dates and shared curation do not permit a temporal independence claim. Availability of annotations for an unseen drug is an explicit assumption, rather than complete chemical cold start.')
p('The main classifier was XGBoost, using 100 trees, depth 5, learning rate 0.1, subsample and column subsample 0.9, and the multiclass soft-probability objective [24]. Linear SVM (C = 1) and random forest (100 trees, minimum leaf size 2, square-root feature subsampling) were fitted as comparators [25]. Parameters were fixed in advance and no test labels were used for fitting or tuning. This is an implementation of standard algorithms, not the graph or attention architecture in CSK-DDI.')
h2('4.3 Splits and evaluation')
p('For each of three seeds (11, 22, 33), an 80:20 stratified random pair split was created. Almost all test drugs also occurred in training, although 4-8 test drugs per random split did not; the split is therefore not strictly restricted to seen drugs. Separately, 20% of the 551 drugs were held out at random. The cold-start training set contains only pairs of non-held-out drugs. S1 contains test pairs with one held-out drug and one drug actually observed in training; S2 contains pairs of two held-out drugs. Pairs with a non-held-out drug that nevertheless did not appear in training were excluded from S1. No test pair occurs in training. All three feature configurations and algorithms use identical partitions.')
p('As an additional identity audit, isomeric canonical SMILES were generated for all 551 sampled drug IDs. All 551 strings were distinct, and none of the 110 held-out drug IDs in each cold split had the same canonical isomeric SMILES as a training drug. This is an exact-string check under the selected representation; it does not rule out salts, tautomers, close analogues, shared molecular scaffolds or other forms of chemical similarity.')
p('Random tests contained 1,864 rows; cold-start training sets contained 5,426-6,066 rows, S1 tests 2,919-3,367, and S2 tests 334-514. All eight classes appeared in every test split. The main metric, macro-F1, is the unweighted mean of the eight per-class F1 scores; accuracy and balanced accuracy are supplementary. Reported values are means and sample standard deviations across three seeds. These partitions overlap and their results are not independent trials; the standard deviation is descriptive, not a confidence interval. Random and cold-start comparisons also change training size and test composition, so performance differences cannot be attributed solely to one factor.')
h2('4.4 Statistical analysis')
p('The principal outcome was macro-F1 using combined structure and target features; secondary metrics included accuracy, macro-precision, macro-recall, macro-specificity and macro one-versus-rest AUROC. For AUROC, each of the eight classes was treated in turn as positive versus the other seven, and the eight AUROCs were averaged. The SVM uses uncalibrated decision scores; the other models use class scores. Thus AUROC measures ranking, not calibration. Three fixed seeds yield descriptive means and standard deviations only; ordinary t-tests or Wilcoxon tests across these overlapping splits do not supply reliable independent replicates [27].')
p('We explored paired differences in mean macro-F1 using 3,000 drug-node resamples (seed 20261108). For each resample, 551 drug identities were drawn with replacement and each test pair received the product of its two drug multiplicities. The same node weights were applied across models and the three fixed splits; macro-F1 was computed within each split and then averaged. Models were not refitted. The 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the paired differences are presented as exploratory resampling ranges, not validated confidence intervals for future populations. Resamples lacking a true class in any split were discarded. The double-unseen-drug ranges were suppressed because 730 of 3,000 draws (24.3%) lacked a class. This post-hoc safeguard, the class-capped sampling and the unknown dependence structure limit interpretation of all ranges.')
p('As a complementary assessment of accuracy, each test-pair graph was reduced to a deterministic maximum-cardinality set of pairs sharing no drug; the selection used drug identities only, without predictions or outcomes. For every split and pair of models, the exact two-sided McNemar test was applied to discordant correct/incorrect predictions [28]. Holm adjustment covered all 27 tests (three scenarios, three seeds and three model contrasts) [29]. McNemar tests accuracy, not macro-F1 or AUROC. Non-overlap of test drugs reduces one dependence pathway but does not establish independence: pairs may share curation sources and all predictions condition on fitted models. Thus p-values are exploratory and do not establish generalisable superiority.')
h('5. Results and Discussion')
h2('5.1 Descriptive data and primary model')
fig('class_counts.png',12.8,5.5,'Figure 2. Counts in the capped eight-class analysis sample. Event IDs are numeric labels; the downloaded files do not provide a verified description-to-ID mapping.')
rows=[['Evaluation','Representation','Macro-F1','Accuracy']]
for sc,label in [('random','Random pair'),('cold_s1','One unseen drug'),('cold_s2','Two unseen drugs')]:
 for rp,rlabel in [('structure','Structure'),('target','Targets'),('structure_target','Structure + targets')]: rows.append([label,rlabel,val(sc,'xgboost',rp),val(sc,'xgboost',rp,'accuracy')])
story.append(Paragraph('Table 1. XGBoost results across three seeds (mean ± sample SD).',st['Capx']));tab(rows,[3.4,4.0,3.7,3.7])
p('With random pair splitting, the primary model achieved macro-F1 '+val('random','xgboost','structure')+' using structure alone and '+val('random','xgboost','structure_target')+' using both inputs. For S1, the corresponding scores were '+val('cold_s1','xgboost','structure')+' and '+val('cold_s1','xgboost','structure_target')+'. These are small descriptive differences in a single sampled benchmark; with only three overlapping partitions they do not establish a statistically reliable benefit from fusion.')
p('The two-unseen-drug condition was substantially more difficult for structure and fused features. Target-only XGBoost achieved '+val('cold_s2','xgboost','target')+', higher than structure-only ('+val('cold_s2','xgboost','structure')+') and the combined model ('+val('cold_s2','xgboost','structure_target')+'). This is an exploratory result, not evidence that target knowledge universally improves out-of-distribution prediction: the configurations have different feature dimensions, the same untuned tree settings, and S2 contains relatively few pairs with variable drug and class composition.')
h2('5.2 Comparison with alternative algorithms')
rows=[['Model and input','Random pair','One unseen','Two unseen']]
for model,label in [('xgboost','XGBoost'),('linear_svm','Linear SVM'),('random_forest','Random forest')]:
 for rep,short in [('structure','structure'),('target','targets'),('structure_target','combined')]: rows.append([label+' - '+short,val('random',model,rep),val('cold_s1',model,rep),val('cold_s2',model,rep)])
story.append(Paragraph('Table 2. Macro-F1 comparison by input, evaluation and classifier (mean ± SD).',st['Capx']));tab(rows,[4.5,4.05,4.05,3.8])
fig('performance.png',16,7.2,'Figure 3. Macro-F1 by model, representation and test setting. Bars show the mean across three splits and error bars one sample standard deviation. These are not independent external datasets.')
p('With combined features, the linear SVM achieved macro-F1 '+val('random','linear_svm','structure_target')+' in the random split, '+val('cold_s1','linear_svm','structure_target')+' in S1 and '+val('cold_s2','linear_svm','structure_target')+' in S2. The random forest achieved '+val('random','random_forest','structure_target')+', '+val('cold_s1','random_forest','structure_target')+' and '+val('cold_s2','random_forest','structure_target')+' in the respective settings. Among the combined-input models considered here, SVM had the highest mean in random splits, whereas random forest had the highest mean in S1. These descriptive rankings are conditional on the fixed settings and three overlapping partitions and should not be interpreted as statistical proof of superiority.')
p('The baseline comparisons show that the ranking depends on both representation and test setting; a single random-split score is therefore insufficient to select a model for a new-drug application. The same held-out drugs are used across feature configurations within each seed, supporting a paired descriptive comparison. However, fixed hyperparameters, unequal cold-start training sizes and unknown data provenance can influence rankings.')
h2('5.3 Statistical comparison of the three models')
p('Table 3 adds precision, recall, specificity and one-versus-rest AUROC to the model comparison. The same three splits and combined features were used for all models. Macro-specificity is an average of eight one-versus-rest specificities and should not be mistaken for a binary interaction-safety specificity. AUROC can be high even when the predicted class is wrong, because it evaluates ranking across thresholds rather than the single chosen class.')
rows=[['Test','Model','Accuracy','Precision','Recall','Specificity','Macro-F1','AUROC']]
for sc,short in [('random','Random'),('cold_s1','S1'),('cold_s2','S2')]:
 for model,label in [('xgboost','XGB'),('random_forest','RF'),('linear_svm','SVM')]:
  v=ext.loc[(sc,model)]; cols=['accuracy','macro_precision','macro_recall','macro_specificity','macro_f1','macro_auroc_ovr'];rows.append([short,label,*[f"{v[(c,'mean')]:.3f} ± {v[(c,'std')]:.3f}" for c in cols]])
story.append(Paragraph('Table 3. Multi-class performance, combined features; means ± SD over three overlapping splits.',st['Capx']));tab(rows,[1.6,1.6,2.25,2.25,2.25,2.25,2.25,2.15])
p('On random splits, mean macro-F1 was 0.821 for SVM and 0.798 for random forest, whereas mean macro-AUROC was 0.961 and 0.970, respectively. This reversal illustrates that the metrics answer different questions. Macro-AUROC is not a clinical effectiveness estimate and, for SVM, is calculated from decision scores rather than class probabilities.')
rows=[['Test','Difference A - B','Mean difference','Exploratory 95% range']]
for sc,short in [('random','Random'),('cold_s1','S1'),('cold_s2','S2')]:
 for a,b,lab in [('xgboost','random_forest','XGBoost - RF'),('xgboost','linear_svm','XGBoost - SVM'),('random_forest','linear_svm','RF - SVM')]:
  q=paired[(paired.scenario==sc)&(paired.model_a==a)&(paired.model_b==b)].iloc[0]; interval=f"[{q.node_range_low:.3f}, {q.node_range_high:.3f}]" if bool(q.report_range) else 'Suppressed: incomplete classes';rows.append([short,lab,f"{q['difference']:.3f}",interval])
story.append(Paragraph('Table 4. Paired macro-F1 differences: 3,000 node-resampling draws, conditional on fitted models.',st['Capx']));tab(rows,[2.4,4.2,3.0,6.5])
fig('paired_node_resampling.png',16,6.2,'Figure 4. Paired macro-F1 differences (first minus second model). Bars are exploratory 2.5th-97.5th percentile ranges, conditional on fitted models; S2 bars are suppressed because 24.3% of draws lacked a class. Individual ranges are not multiplicity-adjusted.')
p('For S1, the observed mean difference between XGBoost and random forest was -0.031, with a conditional node-resampling range of [-0.052, -0.012]. This sensitivity analysis suggests a possible advantage for the forest on these fixed predictions, but it is not a validated population confidence interval or a multiplicity-adjusted hypothesis test. For S2 the observed differences are small and class omissions render the corresponding resampling ranges too unstable to report.')
p('The exact McNemar results in Table 5 assess correctness in smaller matched subsets, not the macro-F1 estimates in Table 4. The smallest adjusted p-value was 0.039 for random-split seed 22 (RF versus SVM; 10 RF-only correct and 31 SVM-only correct out of 236 matched pairs). The other 26 adjusted p-values exceeded 0.05. This isolated retrospective result depends on an unverified independence assumption and a matching-selected subset; the analysis was not preregistered. It cannot establish a generally superior model.')
rows=[['Test / seed','Comparison A vs B','n','A only / B only','p','Holm p']]
for sc,short in [('random','Random'),('cold_s1','S1'),('cold_s2','S2')]:
 for seed in [11,22,33]:
  for a,b,lab in [('xgboost','random_forest','XGB / RF'),('xgboost','linear_svm','XGB / SVM'),('random_forest','linear_svm','RF / SVM')]:
   q=matched[(matched.scenario==sc)&(matched.seed==seed)&(matched.model_a==a)&(matched.model_b==b)].iloc[0];rows.append([f'{short} / {seed}',lab,str(int(q.matched_n)),f'{int(q.a_only_correct)} / {int(q.b_only_correct)}',f'{q.p_raw:.3f}',f'{q.p_holm_27:.3f}'])
story.append(PageBreak());story.append(Paragraph('Table 5. Exploratory exact McNemar tests on drug-disjoint matched subsets; Holm adjustment across 27 tests.',st['Capx']));tab(rows,[2.5,3.2,1.3,3.8,2.0,2.0])
p('A only and B only are counts of discordant matched pairs correctly classified by the first or second model, respectively. Maximum matching depends only on drug identities, but individual test pairs may still be dependent for other reasons, and all splits reuse source data. The matched subset has a different composition from the full test set. Consequently these p-values are not direct tests of the macro-F1 differences.')
h2('5.4 Relationship to previous research')
p('The decline from random-pair evaluation to new-drug evaluation is consistent in direction with the concern raised by DDI-Ben about distribution changes [20]. It is not a replication of that benchmark: our drug IDs were held out randomly and the training size and test composition also changed. We did not impose a scaffold-disjoint or temporal split as explored in other studies [15,17]. Consequently, the results cannot isolate a unique cause of deterioration or estimate prospective performance.')
p('The target-only model sometimes outperformed simple concatenation on S2, an observation that complicates the assumption that more features always improve prediction. It does not contradict graph-based multimodal work [6,7,9,13,19]: their encoders, information sources, task definitions and evaluation protocols are different. Our comparison does not isolate the contribution of biological mechanisms, establish the quality of target annotations, or show that a graph architecture is unnecessary.')
p('The cancer-specific SMVSNN study [16] and the organ-level OrganADR study [1] illustrate distinct clinical motivations. The present analysis does not provide tumour-specific labels, organ outcomes, patient characteristics or evidence for a new therapeutic target. Likewise, PK-DDIP evaluated a quantitative exposure endpoint with external sources [5], whereas our label-only benchmark cannot establish changes in exposure. Cross-paper numerical ranking would therefore be inappropriate.')
h2('5.5 Relevance and limitations')
p('The sampled pairs include a small number involving fluorouracil, doxorubicin or letrozole, medicines relevant to oncology, but the overall benchmark was not assembled as a breast-cancer cohort. The numbers of oncology-related held-out examples are too small and uneven for a reliable disease-specific analysis. Thus the model is an exploratory example of drug-safety modelling in drug discovery, not evidence of a new breast-cancer target or of patient benefit.')
p('First, all records are known interactions and only eight common event IDs are considered. A deployed screening system would need curated negatives or an explicit positive-unlabelled design and event descriptions, neither of which is provided here. Second, data were capped by class and were not sampled to reflect real-world prevalence; accuracy, positive predictive values and calibration would differ in deployment. Third, the same biomedical sources may influence both targets and labels. Although interaction edges are not fed directly to the model, indirect information leakage or annotation bias cannot be ruled out.')
p('Fourth, a drug held out from the DDI training set is not necessarily a novel molecule: its structure and often its target annotations are known. Prospective discovery with few annotations may therefore be harder. Fifth, pair splitting tests interpolation among common drugs and S2 tests a different, smaller and potentially shifted population. Scaffolds, time periods, databases and clinical sites were not independently held out. Sixth, three seeds and a single sampled set do not capture dataset-construction uncertainty. The experiment did not include patient characteristics, doses, exposure or laboratory measurements, and predicted event IDs cannot be translated to actionable management advice.')
p('Future work should confirm numeric event labels against an authoritative mapping; use a versioned time-split and an independent external database; test unseen chemical scaffolds and missing-annotation scenarios; tune models using training-only nested validation; assess calibration and class-specific errors; and obtain pharmacological or experimental validation. In oncology, a prespecified panel of antineoplastics and relevant concomitant medicines could make the evaluation directly relevant to a defined use case.')
h('6. Conclusions')
p('A reproducible machine learning pipeline was implemented to classify eight recorded DDI event types from drug structures and target annotations. XGBoost and two standard baselines were evaluated under random and drug-held-out splits. Predictive performance decreased substantially in the two-unseen-drug setting, and simple concatenation did not consistently outperform either input alone. The results support careful evaluation of generalisation and input availability; they do not establish safety, clinical efficacy, drug synergy or novel target discovery.')
h('7. References')
